course: dictionary
lesson: 12476
title: Formal words: defray
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: To defray means to pay some or all of the cost of something. Think of it as taking part of a bill off someone's shoulders. It is a formal word, so you will meet it in letters, notices and exams more than in everyday talk.
explain: Read the context: «A small charge will help defray the cost of the trip.» The cost is the money the trip needs, and the small charge pays part of it. The clues are help and cost, so defray must mean to help pay for.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it in: «A small charge will help pay for the cost of the trip.» It still makes sense, so defray is close to pay for. In everyday speech people say «help pay for» or «cover», but defray is the formal choice.
fact: Defray means to pay some or all of the cost of something.
fact: Defray is a verb. Its forms are defrays, defrayed and defraying.
fact: It is formal, and it usually goes with «costs» or «expenses».
fact: Everyday words with a similar meaning are «help pay for» and «cover».
fact: «The club defrayed the costs of the new kit» means the club paid some or all of the cost of the kit.
exercise: «The school will defray the cost of the bus.» What does defray mean here? | To drive the bus fast | To be late for the bus | *To pay for some or all of the cost
exercise: Which phrase is closest in meaning to defray? | Hide from | *Help pay for | Make bigger | Forget about
exercise: Which words usually go with defray? | Songs or games | Colours or shapes | *Costs or expenses
test: «The charity will defray the expenses of the summer camp.» What does the charity do? | Sings songs at the camp | Cancels the camp because it is raining | *Pays some or all of the costs | Closes the camp for ever
test: Mum says, «We can defray the cost of your ticket.» What is she telling you? | *We can pay for part or all of your ticket | We will sell your ticket | You must walk there instead | Your ticket is no longer needed
exam: Which sentence uses defray correctly? | I defray my favourite song every morning. | *The parents agreed to defray the cost of the trip. | The dog will defray the ball. | Please defray the sunshine on the field.
